<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limes Germanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhine-Danube frontier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Limes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> was the fortified frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the land between them. It consisted of forts, watchtowers, walls and palisades guarding Roman provinces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. It is a prominent example of the defensive frontier systems that shaped Roman military strategy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus.jpg" alt="Limes Germanicus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Weinandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saalburg_-_Haupteingang_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
